% ============================================================================
% eye-part-property — a descriptive PROPERTY of three eye parts, decoded from
% parenthetical spans already sitting unused inside `eye-parts.adj`'s own
% already-quoted NEI source sentences (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `eye-parts.adj`
% (`eye_part_function(part, function)`, cornea/pupil/iris/lens/retina/
% optic_nerve → bends_light/lets_in_light/controls_light/focuses_light/
% turns_light_into_signals/carries_signals_to_brain): that table's own header
% already quotes per-row NEI spans (verbatim apart from line-wrapping and the
% U+00A0 of its retina and optic-nerve spans) that each name a part's FUNCTION
% (what it DOES to light) -- but three of those very same quoted spans also
% carry a parenthetical or descriptive clause naming a PROPERTY of the part
% (what it IS or looks like), a fact the function/description schema had no
% column for:
%
%   cornea → dome_shaped
%     "The cornea is shaped like a dome and bends light to help the eye
%      focus."
%
%   iris → colored_part_of_eye
%     "The iris (the colored part of the eye) controls how much light the
%      pupil lets in."
%
%   retina → light_sensitive_layer
%     "When light hits the retina (a light-sensitive layer of tissue at the
%      back of the eye), special cells called photoreceptors turn the light
%      into electrical signals."
%
% "Property" (a static descriptive fact about the part itself) is categorically
% distinct from the parent table's "function" (an action the part performs on
% light) -- the cornea's function is bends_light, but its property is that it
% IS dome-shaped; the two facts do not collapse into one column. New
% `eye_part_property(part, property)` table decodes each part's own row:
%
%   cornea, iris, retina → their own single property atom, one row each.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? eye_part_property(iris, $Property)  % colored_part_of_eye
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `eye_part_property(part, property)` carrying the citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the property
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any part
% that is not one of these three (it never invents a property). The query also
% runs BACKWARD -- bind a property and recall the part that has it:
%
%     ? eye_part_property($P, dome_shaped)  % cornea
%
% A recall on `lens` -- a real, already-tabled eye part in `eye-parts.adj`, but
% whose own quoted span ("The lens works together with the cornea to focus
% light correctly on the retina.") states only its function, never a
% descriptive property comparable to "dome-shaped" or "colored" -- correctly
% abstains rather than inventing a property for it. The same holds for `pupil`
% (only "an opening" — a role, not a property) and `optic_nerve` (only that it
% carries signals) — deliberately left unrowed here.
%
% Provenance: the SAME NEI "How the Eyes Work" page `eye-parts.adj` cites.
% This paragraph used to say the spans were reproduced "byte-for-byte" with
% "no new WebFetch"; they are now measured against the page itself, and the
% retina quote above was NOT byte-for-byte: the page writes U+00A0 (NO-BREAK
% SPACE) on both sides of "retina", which a comment cannot show. In the
% ordinary-space form it occurs zero times; the row carries the page's form.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the iris sentence, the primary source of all three answers. Each
% quoted sentence above names its part and its property, so every row now
% takes its own as a `source`. The envelope is now the page's framing
% sentence, the one `eye-parts.adj` uses, which names no part:
%
%     "All the different parts of your eyes work together to help you see."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the three row sentences (retina in its page form) and
% the envelope each occur exactly once, each inside a `<p>`. Every row's page
% is the envelope's, so no row restates a `locator` line or `trust`
% (ADJ-TABLES §4). `trust authoritative` -- NEI is a primary U.S. government
% NIH institute.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table eye_part_property {
    columns part, property

    row (cornea, dome_shaped) {
        source "The cornea is shaped like a dome and bends light to help the eye focus."
    }
    row (iris, colored_part_of_eye) {
        source "The iris (the colored part of the eye) controls how much light the pupil lets in."
    }
    row (retina, light_sensitive_layer) {
        source "When light hits the retina (a light-sensitive layer of tissue at the back of the eye), special cells called photoreceptors turn the light into electrical signals."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE IRIS ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of all
    % three answers. It is now the page's framing sentence (the same one
    % eye-parts.adj uses), which names no part and no property. Every row
    % overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "All the different parts of your eyes work together to help you see."
    locator "https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/healthy-vision/how-eyes-work"
    trust authoritative
}
